-- Row il-47. EXPECTED RED: thirteen assertions, all failing, all about the six citations of Schedule B. The count (13) is declared in check.sh and in encoding.json.
-- Each states what the citing words need: that the schedule the letter B names in the Law of 1995 is the schedule the regulation means, and stands in the official text today.
-- The official text says the schedule under that letter is the Treasury allocation schedule (Sefer HaChukim 1522 p. 299) and that it was repealed from 1 January 2016 (Sefer HaChukim 2511 pp. 248 and 249),
-- so each fails. The thirteenth states that item 3 of that schedule exists; it has items 1 and 2 only. Nothing here is edited to make it pass.

§ `The defects: the citing words need a schedule that is not the one printed under that letter`

-- 1. The description of the target is not the description of what the citing words point at: survivors pension regs r 2, item 1 of Schedule B.
#ASSERT `the citation points at what it says` (`reference: survivors pension regs r 2, item 1 of Schedule B`)
-- 2. The description of the target is not the description of what the citing words point at: vocational training regs r 1, item 1 of Schedule B.
#ASSERT `the citation points at what it says` (`reference: vocational training regs r 1, item 1 of Schedule B`)
-- 3. The description of the target is not the description of what the citing words point at: vocational training regs r 1, item 3 of Schedule B.
#ASSERT `the citation points at what it says` (`reference: vocational training regs r 1, item 3 of Schedule B`)
-- 4. The description of the target is not the description of what the citing words point at: proportional pension regs r 1(2), Schedule B.
#ASSERT `the citation points at what it says` (`reference: proportional pension regs r 1(2), Schedule B`)
-- 5. The description of the target is not the description of what the citing words point at: grants regs r 4(a), Schedule B.
#ASSERT `the citation points at what it says` (`reference: grants regs r 4(a), Schedule B`)
-- 6. The description of the target is not the description of what the citing words point at: grants regs r 17, Schedule B.
#ASSERT `the citation points at what it says` (`reference: grants regs r 17, Schedule B`)

-- The target does not stand in the official text on a day long after the repeal.
-- 7. survivors pension regs r 2, item 1 of Schedule B.
#ASSERT `the target stands in the official text on` (`reference: survivors pension regs r 2, item 1 of Schedule B`) (Date 10 11 2026) EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- 8. vocational training regs r 1, item 1 of Schedule B.
#ASSERT `the target stands in the official text on` (`reference: vocational training regs r 1, item 1 of Schedule B`) (Date 10 11 2026) EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- 9. vocational training regs r 1, item 3 of Schedule B.
#ASSERT `the target stands in the official text on` (`reference: vocational training regs r 1, item 3 of Schedule B`) (Date 10 11 2026) EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- 10. proportional pension regs r 1(2), Schedule B.
#ASSERT `the target stands in the official text on` (`reference: proportional pension regs r 1(2), Schedule B`) (Date 10 11 2026) EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- 11. grants regs r 4(a), Schedule B.
#ASSERT `the target stands in the official text on` (`reference: grants regs r 4(a), Schedule B`) (Date 10 11 2026) EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- 12. grants regs r 17, Schedule B.
#ASSERT `the target stands in the official text on` (`reference: grants regs r 17, Schedule B`) (Date 10 11 2026) EQUALS JUST TRUE

-- 13. The item cited in regulation 1 of the vocational training regulations does not exist in the schedule the letter names.
#ASSERT (`reference: vocational training regs r 1, item 3 of Schedule B`)'s `the cited item exists in the target as enacted`
